#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# Published-skill immutability guard (K-744).
#
# WHAT THIS ENFORCES
# ------------------
# `skill-vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.md` files at the root of this repo are PUBLISHED
# ARTEFACTS, and this repo publishes, in normative text, that they never
# change:
#
#   specification.html  "Because a version file is immutable, this pin cannot
#                        drift by construction."
#   spec/protocol.md    §14.4 "Published skill files are immutable and
#                        versioned; a change ships a new file."
#   CLAUDE.md           "a published version file is never edited, every change
#                        ships a new one"  (ADR-0012)
#
# An operator's `/.well-known/kiosk.json` may carry a `skill` pin: the versioned
# URL plus the SHA-256 of its bytes. An AI assistant fetches the URL FROM HERE
# and refuses the skill on a hash mismatch — fail-closed, by design. So editing
# a file that is already published does not "update" anything: it silently
# invalidates every pin taken against the previous bytes, and the only symptom
# is assistants declining to transact against operators who did exactly what the
# spec told them to do. The sentence above stops being true the moment it
# happens, and it is a sentence about a supply-chain control.
#
# WHY IT IS A CHECK AND NOT A CONVENTION
# --------------------------------------
# Because the convention already failed once. Commit 5a9fbf5 (2026-08-10) —
# whose stated purpose was to make a frozen skill's pin un-rottable — achieved
# it by rewriting two bullets inside the already-published skill-v0.3.10.md.
# The effect happened to be nil (no demo held the pre-edit hash), which is
# exactly why nothing caught it and why nothing would catch the next one. The
# reference repo's `bin/check-*` guards exist for the same reason; this is the
# same shape, in the repo that owns the files.
#
# THE SECOND INVARIANT: THE ALIAS MAY NOT COME TO REST DIVERGED (K-847)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# This guard's header used to say «editing skill.md (the alias) allowed — the
# working alias is mutable by design», and that sentence sanctioned a practice
# this repo has now retired. Under it, notes were staged in the live alias
# between cuts, and for as long as they sat there `specification.html` and
# `skill.md` were BOTH publishing a false sentence about a supply-chain control:
# «`https://kiosk.tech/skill.md` is the "latest" alias, byte-identical to the
# newest version file». It was false at head for days, and the drift widened
# while it was being decided.
#
# Phil's answer (2026-08-21, K-847 — audit/DECISIONS-LOG.md) was to make the
# sentence TRUE rather than to soften it, and to state the discipline that keeps
# it true:
#
#   1. a skill edit ENDS in a version bump — the alias may not come to rest
#      diverged from the newest cut;
#   2. re-pinning the demos is PART of the change, not follow-up work;
#   3. no cut anything pins is EVER mutated — so when skill changes are
#      foreseen, bump FIRST and make the edits inside the new cut.
#
# WHAT IS ALLOWED AND WHAT IS NOT
# -------------------------------
#   ADDING skill-v0.4.4.md           allowed — that IS the prescribed mechanism
#   editing skill-v0.4.4.md          allowed while it is UNTRACKED (or staged-add
#     before it is committed          and not yet committed) — which is exactly
#                                    the window rule 3's «bump first, then edit
#                                    inside the new cut» works in. THE CUT IS
#                                    FROZEN THE MOMENT IT IS COMMITTED, because
#                                    that is the same wave in which the demos
#                                    are re-pinned to it (rule 2): from then on
#                                    something pins it. The RANGE pass is
#                                    correspondingly forgiving — a branch that
#                                    adds a cut in one commit and amends it in
#                                    the next still shows a net `A` against the
#                                    base, so a cut that has not reached the
#                                    base branch is not frozen against it.
#                                    Deliberately NOT keyed on "is it in the
#                                    base?" here: a local `origin/main` that
#                                    lags by three cuts would then exempt three
#                                    already-pinned files.
#   editing skill.md (the alias)     allowed WHILE WORKING — but the tree must
#                                    come to rest with the alias byte-identical
#                                    to the newest cut, so an alias edit is
#                                    finished only when the cut carries it too.
#   editing skill-v0.4.2.md          FAILS — it is on the base branch
#   deleting skill-v0.3.4.md         FAILS — unpublishing breaks live pins just
#                                    as thoroughly as rewriting
#   renaming a cut                   FAILS (seen as delete + add)
#   alias sha != newest cut sha      FAILS — the two sentences above are
#                                    normative text on a published site
#   alias frontmatter version !=     FAILS — same reason: the version an
#     the newest cut's version         assistant reads must name the bytes it got
#
# HOW IT DECIDES
# --------------
# Three passes, because the useful moment to be stopped is before the commit,
# the authoritative moment is in CI, and the third property is about the tree
# rather than about a diff:
#
#   1. WORKING TREE — `git status` for tracked, modified or deleted cuts. Stops
#      an edit locally. A no-op in CI, where the tree is clean. Untracked and
#      staged-add paths pass: that is a cut being written, not one being
#      rewritten.
#   2. RANGE — `git diff --name-status --no-renames <merge-base> <head>`, i.e.
#      what this push / pull request does to the base branch. Only status `A`
#      passes. `--no-renames` on purpose: a rename decomposes into D + A, and
#      the D is a failure we want to see rather than a status letter to special-
#      case.
#   3. ALIAS AT REST — sha256(skill.md) against sha256(newest cut), plus the
#      frontmatter `version` against that cut's version. Reads the files, not
#      git, so it is the same answer locally and in CI.
#
# "AT REST" MEANS THE TREE THAT GETS PUSHED, not every intermediate commit, and
# that is forced rather than lenient: a cut is frozen the moment it is
# committed, so the cut is necessarily the LAST commit of a skill change and the
# commits before it hold an alias that has moved ahead of it. Run this on the
# head you are about to push.
#
# BASE RESOLUTION, in order: `--base <ref>`, then `$BASE_SHA` (what CI sets from
# `github.event.before` for a push and `pull_request.base.sha` for a PR), then
# `origin/main`. If none of them resolves to a commit this EXITS NON-ZERO and
# says so: a guard that cannot see its base must not report success. The
# all-zeros SHA GitHub sends when a branch is created is treated as "unset".
#
# Stdlib only, no network, no build. ~0.1 s. Requires unshallowed history
# (`actions/checkout` with `fetch-depth: 0`).
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-skill-immutability                 # working tree + origin/main..HEAD
#   bin/check-skill-immutability --base <ref>    # explicit base
#   bin/check-skill-immutability --head <ref>    # explicit head (default HEAD)
#   bin/check-skill-immutability --list          # every published cut + its sha256

require "digest"
require "open3"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
Dir.chdir(ROOT)

# A published cut: `skill-v<anything>.md` at the repo root. Deliberately looser
# than `\d+\.\d+\.\d+` — anything named like a published version file is
# protected, so a future naming shape is covered rather than silently exempt.
CUT = %r{\Askill-v[^/]+\.md\z}

# The "latest" alias. Two published sentences say it carries the newest cut's
# exact bytes; pass 3 is what makes that true rather than aspirational (K-847).
ALIAS = "skill.md"

# Every published cut, oldest first. Sorted NUMERICALLY on the version
# components, not lexically — `skill-v0.3.10.md` is newer than `skill-v0.3.9.md`
# and a string sort says the opposite.
def cuts
  Dir["skill-v*.md"].select { CUT.match?(_1) }.sort_by { |f| f.scan(/\d+/).map(&:to_i) }
end

# The `version:` line of a skill file's YAML frontmatter, unquoted, or nil.
def frontmatter_version(path)
  File.read(path)[/^version:\s*"?([^"\n]+)"?\s*$/, 1]&.strip
end

# `-c log.follow=false` is not decoration. With `log.follow=true` in a user's
# global config (it is, on the machine this repo is maintained from), `git log
# -- skill-v0.3.11.md` follows the path back through the copy from skill.md and
# reports the repo's INITIAL commit as the one that added it. A guard that
# reads git must not inherit the reader's preferences.
def git(*args)
  out, err, status = Open3.capture3("git", "-c", "log.follow=false", *args)
  [out, err, status.success?]
end

def rev_parse(ref)
  return nil if ref.nil? || ref.empty?
  return nil if ref =~ /\A0{7,40}\z/ # GitHub's "no previous commit" sentinel

  out, _err, ok = git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "#{ref}^{commit}")
  ok ? out.strip : nil
end

# ── argv ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
base_arg = nil
head_arg = "HEAD"
list     = false
argv     = ARGV.dup
until argv.empty?
  case (arg = argv.shift)
  when "--base" then base_arg = argv.shift
  when "--head" then head_arg = argv.shift
  when "--list" then list = true
  else
    warn "usage: #{$PROGRAM_NAME} [--base <ref>] [--head <ref>] [--list]"
    exit 2
  end
end

# ── --list: the immutability ledger ──────────────────────────────────────────
if list
  published = cuts
  puts format("%-22s %-10s %-12s %s", "PUBLISHED CUT", "ADDED", "COMMIT", "SHA-256")
  published.each do |cut|
    log, _err, _ok = git("log", "--diff-filter=A", "--format=%h %ad", "--date=short", "--", cut)
    commit, date = log.lines.last.to_s.split(" ", 2).then { |h, d| [h.to_s, d.to_s.strip] }
    puts format("%-22s %-10s %-12s %s", cut, date, commit, Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(cut)))
  end
  if File.exist?(ALIAS)
    puts format("%-22s %-10s %-12s %s", ALIAS, "(alias)", "-", Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(ALIAS)))
  end
  puts
  puts "#{published.size} published cuts. Every one of them is frozen: a change ships a NEW file, " \
       "and #{ALIAS} carries the newest cut's exact bytes."
  puts
end

failures    = []
notes       = []
newest_cut  = cuts.last

# ── Base + head resolution ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
env_base = ENV["BASE_SHA"] || ENV["GITHUB_BASE_SHA"]
base_src, base =
  if base_arg then ["--base #{base_arg}", rev_parse(base_arg)]
  elsif rev_parse(env_base) then ["$BASE_SHA (#{env_base[0, 12]})", rev_parse(env_base)]
  else ["origin/main", rev_parse("origin/main")]
  end

head = rev_parse(head_arg)

# ── Pass 1: the working tree ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `git status --porcelain` XY codes: an untracked file is `??` (a new cut being
# written — allowed), `A` is staged-add (allowed). Anything else on a path that
# matches CUT is a modification or a deletion of something already tracked.
status_out, status_err, status_ok = git("status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=all")
if status_ok
  status_out.each_line do |line|
    code = line[0, 2].to_s
    path = line[3..].to_s.strip.delete_prefix('"').delete_suffix('"')
    # Rename entries read `R  old -> new`; the old path is the one at risk.
    path = path.split(" -> ").first.to_s
    next unless CUT.match?(path)
    next if code == "??"            # a brand-new cut, not yet tracked
    next if code.start_with?("A")   # staged add of a brand-new cut

    verb = code.include?("D") ? "DELETED" : "MODIFIED"
    failures << "working tree: #{path} is #{verb} (git status `#{code.strip}`) — that file is " \
                "already published and every pin taken against it verifies its CURRENT bytes. " \
                "Revert it (`git checkout -- #{path}`) and ship the change as a NEW cut."
  end
else
  failures << "could not read `git status` (#{status_err.strip}) — this guard must not pass " \
              "without having looked."
end

# ── Pass 2: the range this push / PR introduces ──────────────────────────────
if head.nil?
  failures << "could not resolve head ref `#{head_arg}` to a commit."
elsif base.nil?
  failures << "could not resolve a base commit (tried #{base_src}). This guard compares what a " \
              "push or pull request DOES to the base branch, so with no base it can see nothing " \
              "and must not report success. Pass one explicitly: " \
              "`bin/check-skill-immutability --base <ref>`. In CI, set BASE_SHA (and check out " \
              "with fetch-depth: 0 so the base commit is present)."
else
  # merge-base, not the raw base: correct for a fast-forward push (base itself),
  # for a PR merge commit (the base tip), and for a diverged or force-pushed
  # branch, where a raw two-dot diff would report the base branch's own commits
  # as if this branch had made them.
  mb_out, _err, mb_ok = git("merge-base", base, head)
  from = mb_ok ? mb_out.strip : base
  notes << "no merge-base between #{base[0, 12]} and #{head[0, 12]} — diffing from the base " \
           "directly" unless mb_ok

  diff_out, diff_err, diff_ok = git("diff", "--name-status", "--no-renames", from, head)
  if diff_ok
    changed = diff_out.lines.filter_map do |line|
      code, path = line.chomp.split("\t", 2)
      next unless path && CUT.match?(path)

      [code.to_s[0], path]
    end

    changed.each do |code, path|
      next if code == "A" # publishing a NEW cut is the prescribed mechanism

      verb = case code
             when "D" then "DELETES"
             when "M" then "MODIFIES"
             else "changes (git status `#{code}`)"
             end
      failures << "#{from[0, 12]}..#{head[0, 12]} #{verb} the already-published #{path}. " \
                  "Published skill cuts are immutable (protocol.md §14.4, specification.html: " \
                  "\"this pin cannot drift by construction\"): an operator's `skill` pin verifies " \
                  "that file's SHA-256, so changing or removing it breaks every assistant holding " \
                  "the old hash — fail-closed and invisible from the operator's side. Ship the " \
                  "change as a NEW skill-v*.md and re-pin, exactly as ADR-0012 prescribes."
    end

    added = changed.count { |code, _| code == "A" }
    notes << "range #{from[0, 12]}..#{head[0, 12]}: #{added} new cut(s) published, " \
             "#{changed.size - added} existing cut(s) touched"
  else
    failures << "could not diff #{from}..#{head} (#{diff_err.strip}) — if this is CI, check out " \
                "with `fetch-depth: 0`; a shallow clone does not contain the base commit."
  end
end

# ── Pass 3: the alias, at rest (K-847) ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# The two sentences this enforces are NORMATIVE text on a published site:
#   specification.html  "`https://kiosk.tech/skill.md` is the "latest" alias,
#                        byte-identical to the newest version file"
#   skill.md            "`https://kiosk.tech/skill.md` is the "latest" alias
#                        with identical content"
# Phil's K-847 answer was to keep them and make them true, which turns them into
# a build invariant: a skill edit is not finished until a cut carries it and the
# alias equals that cut. Reads the FILES, so it says the same thing locally and
# in CI, and it does not care which commit put them there.
if File.exist?(ALIAS)
  if newest_cut.nil?
    failures << "no skill-v*.md cut exists at all, so #{ALIAS} mirrors nothing."
  else
    alias_sha = Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(ALIAS))
    cut_sha   = Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(File.binread(newest_cut))
    if alias_sha == cut_sha
      notes << "alias #{ALIAS} == newest cut #{newest_cut} (#{alias_sha[0, 12]}…)"
    else
      failures << "#{ALIAS} is NOT byte-identical to the newest cut #{newest_cut} " \
                  "(#{alias_sha[0, 12]}… vs #{cut_sha[0, 12]}…). specification.html and " \
                  "#{ALIAS} itself both PUBLISH that it is, about a supply-chain control, so " \
                  "the tree must not come to rest like this. A skill edit ends in a version " \
                  "bump (K-847): cut a new skill-v*.md from the alias, re-pin every consumer, " \
                  "and never edit a cut once it has been committed."
    end

    alias_version = frontmatter_version(ALIAS)
    cut_version   = newest_cut[/skill-v(.+)\.md\z/, 1]
    if alias_version != cut_version
      failures << "#{ALIAS} frontmatter says version #{alias_version.inspect} but the newest cut " \
                  "is #{cut_version.inspect} (#{newest_cut}). An assistant reads the version out " \
                  "of the frontmatter, so it must name the bytes it was handed."
    end
  end
else
  failures << "#{ALIAS} does not exist — the \"latest\" alias is part of what this site publishes."
end

notes.each { puts "  note: #{_1}" }

if failures.empty?
  puts "check-skill-immutability: OK — no published skill-v*.md was modified or removed, " \
       "and #{ALIAS} is byte-identical to #{newest_cut}."
  exit 0
end

warn "\ncheck-skill-immutability FAILED:\n"
failures.each { warn "  x #{_1}" }
warn "\nA published version file is never edited, and the alias never comes to rest diverged " \
     "from the newest cut. Every change ships a NEW skill-vX.Y.Z.md, the demos are re-pinned in " \
     "the same wave, and skill.md is regenerated from it (ADR-0012, K-847). That is what makes " \
     "the spec's \"byte-identical to the newest version file\" true."
exit 1
